<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syncretism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Syncretism is the blending of different religious, philosophical or cultural traditions into new combined forms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>'s conquests encouraged a fusion of Greek and Eastern knowledge, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> absorbed foreign deities into its pantheon. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>, the term describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-ali">Sunni Ali</a>'s mixture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> with indigenous religious practice, which his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askia-muhammad">Askia Muhammad</a> replaced with a more orthodox Islamic legitimacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syncretism.jpg" alt="Syncretism" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jebulon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pluto_Serapis_and_Persephone_Isis_Heraklion_museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The English word is first attested in 1618. It comes from Modern Latin syncretismus, drawing on the Greek synkretismós, supposedly meaning a Cretan federation. This etymology goes back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>'s essay Fraternal Love of the 1st century, which describes the Cretans reconciling their differences and joining in alliance when faced with external dangers. A more likely derivation is from sun- (with) and kerannumi (to mix).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Religious syncretism blends two or more belief systems into a new one, or brings beliefs from unrelated traditions into an existing one. It usually follows contact between cultures, or conquest, when the conquerors do not entirely remove the old beliefs or, especially, practices. In religion it denotes a more integrated merging than eclecticism, which selects elements from different traditions without forming a new, cohesive system.</p><p>Attitudes to the term differ. Adherents of exclusivist or revealed religions often reject the label, seeing the addition of an incompatible belief as a corruption of the original faith. Non-exclusivist systems may feel free to take in other traditions. Véronique Altglas argues that the term presupposes that the traditions combined were once pure, whereas in her view all religions are syncretic to some degree.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Kushite kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-fifth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-fifth Dynasty of Egypt</a>, from 721 to 664 BCE, identified their god Dedun with the Egyptian Osiris. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> rulers identified local deities with Greek gods to strengthen the cohesion of their kingdoms, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> rejected this, regarding the identification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> as the worst blasphemy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> equated its own deities with Greek ones, forming a single Greco-Roman pantheon, and then with the local deities of its provinces.</p><p>In the 16th century the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> proposed the Din-i Ilahi, or Divine Faith, which drew mainly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and also on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>. It had no scriptures or priesthood and fewer than 20 disciples, all chosen by Akbar. Syncretism tends to ease coexistence between different cultures, which recommended it to rulers of multi-ethnic realms, while rejecting it in the name of orthodoxy can build a sense of uncompromised unity in a group.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Syncretism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The interpretatio romana, the Roman habit of absorbing and reinterpreting foreign deities within the Roman pantheon, encouraged a religious <b>syncretism</b> that helped prepare the ground for Christianity's eventual triumph under Constantine I (r. 306–337 CE).</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Their mythology shows some <b>syncretism</b> with Greek and Roman traditions, yet the conception and worship of their divinities was individual and not an adoption of other cultures’ practices.</blockquote><a href="/religion/etruscan-religion">Etruscan religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Such efforts toward <b>syncretism</b> are among the few explicit ones that appear in inscriptions.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hittite-religion">Hittite religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It now coexists and interacts with pan-Mayan <b>syncretism</b>, the 're-invention of tradition' by the Pan-Maya movement, and Christianity in its various denominations.</blockquote><a href="/religion/maya-religion">Maya religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>A process of yorubización emerged, which sought to remove Roman Catholic elements, and critics of it valued Santería's <b>syncretism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/santeria">Santería · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Shoqan Walikhanov wrote that only the names became Islamic while the ideas stayed shamanic, though other scholars now challenge the theory of <b>syncretism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
